<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
  <!--
    What a foreground service costs, declared.

    Android stops a background app from receiving Bluetooth scan results while
    the screen is off, suspends a cached app's network in Doze, and kills the
    process when the app is swiped away. Those are what make the device
    unreachable and a notification undeliverable with a locked phone, and a
    foreground service is the mechanism Android offers for lifting them.

    The type is `specialUse`, and the reason it is not `connectedDevice` is
    worth keeping. From Android 14 a type also requires the app to *hold* one of
    that type's prerequisite permissions, and `connectedDevice` asks for one of
    the Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, NFC or USB permissions. The reader grants the
    Bluetooth ones when they turn the *device* on — so a reader who turns
    notifications on and owns no device holds none of them, and `startForeground`
    threw `SecurityException` out of `onStartCommand` and closed the app. That
    is the common case for notifications, and it was reproduced on an API 35
    emulator.

    `specialUse` is also the more honest word. This service holds no Bluetooth
    and no socket: it keeps covey's own process alive, which is a use Android's
    list does not have a name for, and `specialUse` is the category it offers
    for exactly that. Its permission is a normal one, granted at install, so the
    throw cannot come back. The types with no such prerequisite that do have a
    name — `dataSync` — are the two Android 15 caps at six hours a day, which
    would be a feature that stops working in the evening.

    POST_NOTIFICATIONS is asked for at run time on Android 13 and later; without
    it the service still runs, but its notification is invisible, and a reader
    should be able to see what is holding their phone awake.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

  <application>
    <service
      android:name="farm.dylan.covey.link.CoveyLinkService"
      android:enabled="true"
      android:exported="false"
      android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse">
      <!--
        Android 14 and later want the use spelled out in words as well as in a
        type. This string is read by a person reviewing the app, and it is the
        whole of what the service does.
      -->
      <property
        android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
        android:value="Keeps covey connected to the machines you added, so your device stays reachable and a thread that needs you can say so while the screen is off." />
    </service>
  </application>
</manifest>
